Pull Model

(GitLab Runner installed on target/on-prem server)

✅ Pros
No inbound access required
Target server only needs outbound access to GitLab (more firewall-friendly).
Stronger security posture (network-wise)
No SSH ports or deploy endpoints exposed.
Closer to “zero-trust” model
Server pulls jobs; nothing externally forces execution.
Simpler infra
No jump hosts, SSH key distribution, or remote orchestration needed.
Execution happens locally
Faster deployments (no file transfer over SSH).
Good for restricted environments
Works well in banking/enterprise networks (common in Capco-type setups).
❌ Cons
Security risk of runner
Runner executes pipeline code → effectively remote code execution on server.
Tight coupling
CI logic and production server are directly linked.
Harder to centralize control
Each server needs runner setup, updates, monitoring.
Scaling complexity
Many servers = many runners to manage.
Limited orchestration
Not ideal for multi-server coordinated deployments.
Debugging can be harder
Issues happen directly on prod machine.
🚀 Push Model

(Central GitLab runner pushes via SSH / Ansible / scripts)

✅ Pros
Better separation of concerns
CI runner ≠ production server.
Stronger control & audit
All deployments originate from centralized pipeline runners.
Safer production environment
No CI execution engine installed on prod.
Easier orchestration
Can deploy to multiple servers in sequence or parallel.
Scales better
One runner can manage many servers.
More flexible tooling
Works well with Ansible, Terraform, Kubernetes, etc.
❌ Cons
Requires inbound access
SSH or agent access must be opened → security concern.
Credential management overhead
SSH keys, vaults, rotation, etc.
More moving parts
Jump hosts, bastions, orchestration layers.
Network dependency
If connection fails, deployment fails.
Slower deployments (sometimes)
File transfer + remote execution overhead.
