| Execution    | From CI/CD pipeline          | From agent on server         |
| Connectivity | Requires access *to* servers | Server connects *out*        |
| Security     | Needs SSH/credentials        | Safer in restricted networks |
| Control      | Centralized                  | Decentralized                |
| Network      | Needs inbound access         | Works with outbound-only     |

Core Difference: Deployment Model

============================
GitLab (Push-based)
Deployment is triggered from GitLab CI/CD pipelines.
The runner (or pipeline job) pushes code/artifacts to target servers.
Typically uses:
SSH
Kubernetes API
Deployment scripts (Ansible, etc.)

Flow:

Code commit → Pipeline runs → GitLab Runner → Push to server


Nolio (Pull-based)
Uses agents installed on target servers.
Agents poll the Nolio server for deployment instructions.
When a deployment is triggered:
Agent pulls artifacts and executes locally.
No inbound connection needed

✅ Option 1: GitLab Runner on Target Server (Closest to Pull Model)
Install a GitLab Runner directly on the server.
Configure it with a specific tag.
Jobs run on that server itself.


Option 3: Hybrid with Artifact Repositories
Pipeline pushes artifact to repo (e.g., Nexus, S3)
Servers pull from repo via scheduled jobs


